    "body": "### Go version\n\ngo version go1.27.1 darwin/arm64 (also present on tip, edd77e8488)\n\n\n### What did you do?\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io/fs\"\n\t\"testing/fstest\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n\tfsys := fstest.MapFS{\"site[1]/index.tmpl\": {Data: []byte(\"Hello\")}}\n\tsub, _ := fs.Sub(fsys, \"site[1]\")\n\tdata, err := fs.ReadFile(sub, \"index.tmpl\")\n\tfmt.Printf(\"ReadFile: %q, %v\\n\", data, err)\n\tnames, err := fs.Glob(sub, \"*.tmpl\")\n\tfmt.Printf(\"Glob: %q, %v\\n\", names, err)\n}\n```\n\n### What did you see happen?\n\n```\nReadFile: \"Hello\", <nil>\nGlob: [], <nil>\n```\n\nWith a directory named `site[`, Glob returns `syntax error in pattern` for the same pattern. With `site*` or `site?` and a sibling directory that also matches, it returns `invalid result from inner fsys Glob`. The same happens with `os.DirFS`, where `template.ParseFS(sub, \"*.tmpl\")` fails with `pattern matches no files`.\n\n### What did you expect to see?\n\n```\nGlob: [\"index.tmpl\"], <nil>\n```\n\n[`subFS.Glob`](https://github.com/golang/go/blob/edd77e8488f86768ab479ba7f03a597bff4a867d/src/io/fs/sub.go#L150) prepends the directory name to the pattern without escaping it, so `path.Match` treats the name as a pattern. The generic `fs.Glob` (used when the `GlobFS` method is not available) returns the expected result for the same Sub. `testing/fstest.TestFS`, which also exercises `fs.Sub`, reports it too: for a `MapFS` with `d[1/hello.txt` and `dz/hello.txt` it fails with `testing fs.Sub(fsys, d[1): Glob(`*a*`): syntax error in pattern`.\n",
